<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Imamate of Futa Jallon</h1><p class="st-meta">1725 CE – 1896 CE (approx.) · capital Timbo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Fulbe Islamic state in the Guinea highlands, created by jihad in the 1720s, which rotated power between two dynasties and was the model for later West African imamates.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a> pastoralists had moved into the Futa Jallon highlands over several centuries. Around 1725 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> led by the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karamokho-alfa">Karamokho Alfa</a> (Ibrahima Sambegu) overthrew the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalunka">Jallonke</a> rulers. After his death the warrior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahima-sori">Ibrahima Sori</a> completed the conquest in the 1770s and became almamy, building a theocratic state with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbo">Timbo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The almamy was chosen alternately from two families, the Alfaya descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karamokho-alfa">Karamokho Alfa</a> and the Soriya from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahima-sori">Ibrahima Sori</a>, an arrangement that caused frequent disputes. A council of elders sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugumba">Fugumba</a>, the religious centre, and the land was divided into provinces called diwe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Qur'anic</a> schooling was widespread, and the economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, farming by slaves and trade with the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-pongo">Rio Pongo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> signed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> treaties from 1881 and exploited rivalries between the two houses. In 1896 French troops defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bokar-biro">Almamy Bokar Biro</a> at Poredaka, and Futa Jallon was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-guinea">French Guinea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imamate-of-futa-jallon.jpg" alt="Imamate of Futa Jallon" width="320" height="234"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Improved_Flag_of_the_Imamate_of_Futa_Djallon_(1896-1912).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1725 CE – 1896 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Timbo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karamokho Alfa, Ibrahima Sori, Bokar Biro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pular</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guinea">Guinea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Futa Jallon, Fouta Djallon, Futa Djallon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=imamate-of-futa-jallon" aria-label="Imamate of Futa Jallon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4354 1781 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imamate of Futa Jallon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4674 2037L5054 728M4674 2037L4622 2079"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M4622 2079h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4674 2037h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4674 2037h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Timbo. <a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=imamate-of-futa-jallon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
